# Certificates

- `Manager` owns the canonical game pair and stable read-only per-server mounts. Only mount a server's own directory. Shared modes `custom` and `acme` use the same staged generation mechanism. `Ensure` checks validity before a start. Self-signed game pairs remain independent, short-lived and pinned.
- Game mode changes stage complete pairs outside all mounts. One durable settings write commits the mode and generation. Publication is independent of request cancellation after commit. Startup resumes committed stages. Publish the key first and the certificate last; the engine rejects a transient mismatch and retries.
- `Automation` owns account settings, managed panel TLS and ACME reconciliation. It acquires its operation gate before the game manager lock. Never hold the game lock across CA traffic. Host changes and publishing an issued pair serialize through `ChangeHost` and a host recheck. Panel handshakes use an immutable snapshot. Panel mode commits only after every requested pair is saved.
- One ACME pair per identifier is shared by every active use. Read storage at startup and during maintenance, including after crashes. CA traffic must be limited to active modes or explicit jobs with agreed terms. Do not enable on-demand TLS, DNS-01 or a fallback issuer. IP requests use the shortlived profile.
- Only answer challenges started by the daemon, with the exact HTTP challenge host and path or the matching TLS-ALPN SNI. Never delegate unknown HTTP tokens to CertMagic's blind challenge handler. Treat CA text as untrusted. Log and persist only bounded problem summaries and controlled lifecycle messages. Never log private key material.
- ACME and panel files are private and never mounted. Shared canonical and staged game keys use 0600. Game mount keys use 0640 with the existing group policy and certificates use 0644. Windows uses inherited ACLs.
- Verify with `go test ./internal/certs/... ./internal/httpapi/...`, the Pebble gate in AGENTS.md, and the Docker-gated server tests for mount replacement.
